Title: MYTH OF THE STONES: THE SEVEN STONES
Credit: Draft 1 — PROPOSED, not approved
Notes: ACT TWO of three. Continues from ACT_1.fountain.

FADE IN:

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH — MORNING

Rain has stopped. A thin light lies across the floor. Lyra is awake on the sofa, the grey stone in her lap, tracing the hairline crack with a fingertip. The crack is almost gone.

Tuan sits at the reading table eating toast over the ruins of his camera, reviewing footage on its tiny screen.

TUAN
Forty minutes of nothing, then three seconds where the dust stops. I'd call it a glitch, except I was standing in it.

LYRA
Don't delete it.

TUAN
I've already backed it up four ways. I'm a coward. It's a gift.

At the window Torin stands exactly where he stood the night before. He has not slept. Auren comes out from the back room, rumpled, with a bruise-coloured look under his eyes, and stops in front of Seraphine, who is pouring tea.

AUREN
I'll go.

SERAPHINE
(not turning)
I know.

AUREN
You knew last night.

SERAPHINE
I knew when you were nine. You were standing on a chair trying to reach a book I'd put too high. You said, "If I can't get it down, who can?" I thought: here it is.

AUREN
(slowly)
You met me when I was nine?

SERAPHINE
Briefly. Your mother brought you in. You were very rude about my filing.

She hands him a cup. For a moment he looks as though he might break. Then he drinks.

LYRA
(sitting up)
Where do we start?

SERAPHINE
(spreading the map again)
The stones sleep where the Guardians left them: seven roads, seven silences. Malachar already holds two. You carry one. Four are loose.

She taps the web of nodes. Four are lit faintly on the old paper: three in a line to the east, and one set apart, far to the north.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
The nearest is the Crystal Depths. Beyond that, the Kingdom of Ash. The last two lie in the Eternal Temple, which I would very much prefer you reached last.

TORIN
(from the window)
And before any of that, the Citadel.

Everyone looks at him.

TORIN (CONT'D)
(a shrug)
Every Warden is taught it. The Guardian Citadel holds the records. If Ms Seraphine wants the boy to know what he is, that's where she'll take him.

SERAPHINE
(softly)
That is exactly where.

INT. A RAILWAY STATION LAVATORY — DAY

A cramped cubicle. Torin sits on the closed lid of a toilet with a cheap phone in his hand. He types, deletes, types again. Finally:

TORIN (V.O.)
(typed, reading aloud)
"Citadel. Three days. They have the first. The boy is real."

His thumb hovers over send. He closes his eyes. He thinks of something, a boy in a shelter, a hand on a shoulder, a man's voice saying come in out of the rain.

He sends it.

TORIN
(whisper)
Forgive me.

He wipes the phone clean, snaps the SIM, and flushes the pieces.

EXT. THE KHARESH MOUNTAINS — DAY (MONTAGE)

A convoy of two jeeps climbs a switchback road above a cloud-filled valley. Seraphine drives the lead, humming. In the back seat, Tuan videotapes the scenery; Lyra argues with her notebook; Auren sleeps against the window with his hand wrapped around something small in his lap.

A rail tunnel, abandoned, under a mountain. A plaque: "CLOSED 1911". Seraphine produces a heavy iron key from her cardigan and opens a door that is not on the plaque.

INT. THE GUARDIAN CITADEL — ENTRANCE HALL — DAY

A staircase carved straight down through living rock, wide enough for ten, lit from below by a faint blue glow. Their footsteps echo. The air smells of cold stone and old paper.

At the bottom, a hall that does not end. Columns of grey granite rise to a ceiling lost in dark. Along the walls, in alcoves, thousands of small oil-lamps burn, lit by no hand, tended by no one.

TUAN
(whisper)
Who's been paying the electricity?

SERAPHINE
Nobody. The Lamp does it. It is rather proud.

A single lamp, higher than the rest, brightens as she passes.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
(a soft, androgynous voice; courteous; very old)
Keeper. It has been ninety years.

SERAPHINE
(fondly)
I'm sorry. I was busy.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
You brought visitors.

SERAPHINE
I brought one.

The Lamp's flame bends toward Auren. It trembles.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
(awed)
She has returned.

AUREN
Who has?

THE LAMP (V.O.)
Forgive me. Your face.

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEVEN — CONTINUOUS

The nave opens into a circular chamber. Seven colossal statues stand in a ring, each ten metres tall, each carved from a different stone, each with a hand raised and open as though releasing something.

Auren walks slowly beneath them. At the third he stops.

It is a tall woman, her hair tied back, a tool-belt at her hip, a plain grey tunic. Her face is weathered, kind, fierce, tired.

It is, to the line of the jaw, Auren's face.

TUAN
(whisper)
That's you.

AUREN
No.

LYRA
It's your face.

AUREN
(tracing the plinth with a fingertip)
It's hers.

Carved in the plinth, in a script he has never seen and yet can somehow read:

ITHREN OF THE GREY STONE. SHE WHO TOOK THE FIRST FROM THE AIR.

LYRA
(very quietly)
You can read that.

AUREN
(with a breath, half laughing, half horrified)
I can read it.

Seraphine, behind him, watches his face with an expression no one sees.

SERAPHINE
Ithren was a metalworker before she was a Guardian. Her stone was Memory. She had a gift for remembering what people would rather forget. It is why she kept the Citadel's records, and why she took the stone out of the Loom's path. She was the only one who understood what was about to happen.

AUREN
And Malachar?

SERAPHINE
(a pause)
Malachar was her friend.

AUREN
He was —

SERAPHINE
The oldest she had. They built the Loom together. And when it went wrong, when the sky tore, she put him to sleep.

AUREN
(slowly)
She knocked him out.

SERAPHINE
She stunned him with her stone. So he would not undo what they had done, or make it worse. Then she and the others hid him where no one would ever look. For four thousand years.

LYRA
(a half-whisper)
Four thousand.

SERAPHINE
He woke some three centuries ago. The stone in his hand, the one that holds Time, kept him alive. It has been waiting for him ever since.

AUREN
(turning)
And he's been hunting the others since.

SERAPHINE
(nodding)
Not to destroy them. To put them back.

AUREN
(grimly)
That's worse.

TORIN
(from the far side of the hall; very quiet)
Is it?

Everyone turns. Torin has stopped in front of the sixth statue, a heavy-set man with a laughing, half-worn face. He is looking at it, not at them.

TORIN (CONT'D)
(not looking round)
If you had a daughter, and a sky tore and took her, and a stone sat in your hand that might give her back. Would you hide it?

A very long silence.

AUREN
(very gently)
Is that what he told you?

Torin does not answer. Auren does not push. But the question stays in the hall like a coin set on its edge.

INT. THE CITADEL — THE RECORD-ROOM — LATER

A long gallery of shelves. Auren sits at a worktable under a lamp, the grey stone on a stand before him. Lyra beside him with a notepad. Seraphine in the doorway, hands folded.

AUREN
What do I do?

SERAPHINE
Hold it. Don't force anything. Ask what you want to know.

He closes both hands around the stone. His breathing slows. Light climbs his wrists. On the shelves behind him, lamps flare, and every scroll in the room, hundreds, thousands, unrolls a hand's width toward him, as a field of grass leans in a wind.

AUREN
(eyes closed)
Where are they?

The records answer. A map flows across the air above the table, three points of light in the east, one far north. Under each, a name. He reads them aloud.

AUREN (CONT'D)
The Crystal Depths. The Kingdom of Ash. The Eternal Temple. Two stones in the last.

LYRA
(writing)
Two in one place?

SERAPHINE
Consciousness and Balance. They are the two that must never be separated from each other, nor taken at the same time.

LYRA
Why?

SERAPHINE
Because between them they decide who is allowed to ask what the others are for.

Auren opens his eyes. The map dissolves. He is breathing hard.

AUREN
(hoarse)
It hurt.

LYRA
What?

AUREN
Remembering. It's not like a book. It's like being in the room with everyone who died there.

SERAPHINE
(quietly)
Yes. That is the price of Memory.

AUREN
(looking at her)
Does it get easier?

SERAPHINE
(after a long pause)
No. But you get better at standing in the room.

EXT. THE CRYSTAL DEPTHS — A CAVERN MOUTH — DAY

A cleft in the hillside, narrow and shrub-choked. The jeeps stop. Four figures climb out; Seraphine hangs back by the vehicles with a flask of tea.

SERAPHINE
I'll stay with the cars.

LYRA
You're not coming?

SERAPHINE
I have a bad knee. And I'd be of no use. The Depths want people who haven't forgotten how to be surprised.

TUAN
(grimly)
That's me. I've never not been surprised.

She smiles. She watches them go, and as soon as they are in the dark her smile fades. She lowers her face into one hand and whispers a name that is not her own.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
Ithren. Forgive me. I am going to let him see it for himself.

INT. THE CRYSTAL DEPTHS — DAY

They descend through a crack in the world.

At first it is a cave: wet stone, cold, a trickle of water. Then the walls begin to shine. Veins of a clear mineral thread the rock, then thicken; then they are climbing down a throat of quartz as wide as a cathedral nave, every surface faceted, every facet holding light from a source nobody has yet seen.

A hush of wonder. Tuan swings his camera slowly round. Lyra stops, one hand on a wall.

LYRA
(whisper)
It's growing.

AUREN
What is?

LYRA
Everything. Look, there, those spurs. They didn't have that angle a minute ago.

A crystal spur on the ceiling extends itself, slowly, with a sound like a glass bell being drawn out along a violin string.

TORIN
(from the rear; stripped of irony for the first time)
It's alive.

LYRA
It's responding. To the stone, I think.

She holds the grey stone in her palm. The crystals' light pulses at the same tempo as its glow.

At last the throat opens onto a vast cavern, a lens of clear air hung under a roof of quartz, and across its floor, a chasm. On the far side of the chasm, a pedestal. On the pedestal, a translucent green stone, warm as an old bottle held to a candle.

Between the pedestal and where they stand: nothing but a gulf and, set into the near edge, a cradle of black stone, shaped like a hand.

On the far side, a matching cradle.

TUAN
(peering across)
It's a pair.

LYRA
It's a two-key lock. One at each end of the chasm. You'd need two people.

AUREN
(softly)
One hand can't hold it.

LYRA
What?

AUREN
(reading from a faint memory)
"No one hand holds the whole."

He steps to the near cradle and places his palm in it. A light runs through his fingers into the stone. Across the chasm the matching cradle brightens, but it needs a hand on it.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(turning)
Someone has to go round.

TUAN
Round where? It's a hole in the world.

LYRA
(gazing along the lip)
There. Look.

A thread of crystal arcs across the void, thin as a violin string, strung from one lip to the other. It is the width of a hand.

LYRA (CONT'D)
That's the bridge.

TUAN
That's a thread.

LYRA
It's a thread that goes to the other side.

TORIN
(stepping up beside her)
I'll cross.

LYRA
You?

TORIN
I am the lightest. And you will want a Warden at the far end.

He takes a breath, and, with a courtesy that borders on grace, steps onto the string. It sings under his weight. The crystal light shivers around him. He does not look down.

TORIN (CONT'D)
(calm, to himself, as he walks)
A thing one hand can hold, one hand can drop. A thing two hands hold, neither drops.

Auren watches, white-knuckled, one palm in the cradle. Lyra holds her breath.

Torin reaches the far side. He steps off, brushes his sleeves, walks to the second cradle and, with a small bow to no one, lays his hand in it.

The chasm lights from end to end. A bridge unrolls across it, a ribbon of clear quartz, wide as a road. The green stone on its pedestal lifts a finger's height off its seat and drifts toward the middle of the span as though it had been waiting to be asked.

LYRA
(whisper)
Oh.

AUREN
(releasing his cradle; the light holds)
It stays open.

He walks across the new bridge. Reaches the pedestal. The green stone settles into his palm: Matter. Heavy, solid, cool.

He turns. Torin stands at the far cradle with his hand still in it, watching him. For one long second the two men look at each other.

TORIN
(softly)
It's lighter than I expected.

AUREN
(gripping it)
What is?

TORIN
(a thin smile)
Being trusted.

CUT TO:

INT. THE CRYSTAL DEPTHS — A GALLERY ABOVE THE CAVERN — CONTINUOUS

Torin leaves his cradle and walks to the other side. Lyra and Tuan join Auren at the pedestal. The bridge is still there.

Without warning, the crystals above them dim. A cold, even current of air moves across the cavern, the temperature falling all at once. A shape appears at the mouth of the throat: a man in a grey coat, bareheaded, alone, with a calm face and kind hands.

Malachar walks onto the bridge.

Vesper and Corvin do not follow. They stay at the mouth with the others, hands at their sides, trained.

TUAN
(whisper)
Is that —

TORIN
(very quietly)
Yes.

Malachar stops halfway across. He regards them with an expression of such mild interest that for a moment he might be a lecturer arriving early for a seminar.

MALACHAR
Please. I mean none of you any harm.

LYRA
(raising a trowel like a sword)
You attacked our camp.

MALACHAR
My people did, and I am sorry for it. They were told to be gentle. They were not gentle enough.

LYRA
Hollis could be dead.

MALACHAR
Hollis Reeve is asleep in a hospital in Kharesh City with a headache. I can show you, if you like.
(a beat)
I do not kill. I haven't for a very long time.

Lyra's trowel wavers. Auren steps forward.

AUREN
(low)
You're Malachar.

MALACHAR
(a tender, very tired look)
And you are Ithren's grandson, many times removed. You have her chin. I would know it anywhere.

AUREN
(hoarse)
She put you to sleep.

MALACHAR
She did. I do not blame her. I would have done the same.

AUREN
(a step)
Then why —

MALACHAR
May I show you something?

He lifts his hand, and on it, as if it had been waiting, sits a stone of warm amber. Time.

LYRA
(sharply)
Auren, don't.

MALACHAR
(gently)
It is only a memory. Not a weapon. Hold the other end, if you wish. I will not let go.

A long silence. Auren looks at Lyra. At Torin. At the bridge. Then, slowly, he lifts the grey stone in one hand and, with the other, takes the end of the thread of amber light Malachar holds out to him.

The cavern goes white.

INT. THE AURELITH — A COURTYARD — MORNING (MEMORY)

A white city on a cliff above a green sea. Children run shrieking along a road, trailing paper lanterns. A fountain plays. A man in a plain grey tunic sits on its edge with a small girl on his knee, folding a paper bird.

Auren is there, but unseen: a shadow among the festival crowd, an observer in his own skin.

The man is Malachar, happy, utterly present, entirely himself. The girl laughs at something he says. A woman, her mother, leans over his shoulder and kisses the top of the child's head. The three of them are the whole bright centre of a day.

MALACHAR (V.O.)
(over the memory, quiet)
Her name was Liora. She was six.

The little girl turns. For one second, impossibly, she looks directly at Auren. She holds up the paper bird for him to see.

LIORA
(to Auren; bright, interested)
Do you want to hold it?

Auren reaches out in the memory. His fingers close on nothing.

CUT TO:

INT. THE CRYSTAL DEPTHS — THE BRIDGE — CONTINUOUS

Auren, gasping, is on his knees on the quartz. The amber light has gone. Malachar stands over him, one hand out, steadying, not touching.

LYRA
(running up)
What did you do to him?

AUREN
(hoarse; holding up a palm)
Nothing. He... showed me.

MALACHAR
(softly)
Every morning I wake and the first thing I think is that I am going to see her at breakfast. It takes me nearly a minute to remember. Four thousand years, and it takes me nearly a minute.

He is not performing. He is simply telling them.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
The Guardians told the world they saved it from the Loom. They did not tell the world what the Loom was for.

AUREN
(looking up)
What was it for?

MALACHAR
(a long pause)
That, I think, is what you will have to find out for yourself.

He turns to go. Pauses.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
Ask your Keeper what she knows. Ask her why she was not at the table in the Citadel the day the Lamp spoke her name.

AUREN
(a prickle; slowly)
Seraphine?

MALACHAR
I do not know what she calls herself now.

He looks at Torin across the span. Torin does not meet his gaze. Malachar's eyes rest on him a moment longer than they should.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
(gently)
Keep what you have. It will be easier to take later.

LYRA
(outraged)
Excuse me?

MALACHAR
(with the faintest, saddest smile)
I will not take it today. That is a courtesy. You are very young.

He walks back along the bridge. Vesper and Corvin fall in behind him. Nobody tries to stop them. Nobody can.

TUAN
(whisper)
That was the single most polite hostage situation I have ever been in.

AUREN
(still on his knees)
He's not lying.

LYRA
No.

AUREN
(his voice breaking)
That's what makes it so awful.

EXT. THE CRYSTAL DEPTHS — CAVERN MOUTH — DUSK

They climb out into a violet dusk. The jeeps are where they left them. Seraphine sits on the bonnet of the lead one, sipping tea from a flask, watching them come.

She stands as Auren approaches.

SERAPHINE
You've got it.

AUREN
(holding up the green stone; his voice flat)
I met him.

SERAPHINE
(very still)
Ah.

AUREN
He said to ask you what you know.

Seraphine does not move. A breeze lifts a lock of her hair. For a long moment her face is perfectly unreadable.

SERAPHINE
(carefully)
Then you had better.

AUREN
Not now.

SERAPHINE
(softly)
No. Not now.

She turns to the jeep, opens the door, gets in. Nobody sees the way her hand shakes on the wheel.

INT. THE KINGDOM OF ASH — DAY

A landscape of grey.

Not burnt: ground down. A city the colour of ashes, whose towers and bridges and streets still stand, but are made of ash pressed so fine that they seem to be dissolving as you watch. Wind lifts clouds of it from the rooftops. Where the wind passes, a wall crumbles. Where the ash settles, a new wall rises, brittle, translucent, grey.

SUPER: "KARTH VAEL — A CITY THAT BUILT ITSELF."

Four small figures cross a plaza of dust. Their footprints fill behind them.

TUAN
(muffled through a handkerchief)
Why is it doing that?

SERAPHINE
(gravely)
Because the Aurelith used Creation here before they built the Loom. Alone. Without the others to balance it.

LYRA
What happened?

SERAPHINE
They asked a city to rebuild itself. It never stopped.

A tower to their left lifts, builds, crumbles. A bridge across a canal begins to grow, a lattice of ash reaching toward its far bank, and collapses mid-arc.

AUREN
(quietly)
It's still trying.

SERAPHINE
Yes. For four thousand years. That is what Creation does, with no one to say enough.

In the centre of the plaza stands a fountain, dry, coated in dust. In its basin, among cracked tiles, a stone sits, a pale rose-gold, shimmering with a light that seems to move from within. The wind around it is still.

LYRA
(whisper)
That's Creation.

TORIN
(stopping short of the fountain)
I will go no further.

AUREN
What?

TORIN
(not looking at him)
It is not the stone I mind. It is what it does to people who look at it.

LYRA
(sharply)
We're not going to look at it. We're going to pick it up.

TORIN
(a thin smile)
That is exactly how it begins.

Auren steps to the basin. The wind is silent around him. The stone glimmers.

He reaches out, and in the surface of the stone he sees himself, not as he is but as he might be: standing in a vast white hall, a hundred thousand faces tilted up toward him, the paper bird in his palm. A voice murmurs from everywhere.

CREATION (V.O.)
(sweet, warm, enveloping)
You could make it right. All of it. Everyone you've lost.

AUREN
(trembling)
Mum.

CREATION (V.O.)
Yes. She is there. She is waiting.

A hand closes around Auren's wrist. Lyra, hard, her face inches from his.

LYRA
(urgent)
Auren. Look at me.

AUREN
(dazed)
She's right there.

LYRA
Is she?

AUREN
(wavering)
I —

LYRA
(quiet; fierce)
I lost my father when I was fourteen. I know what it is to see him in a doorway. This is not that. This is the thing in the doorway.

He looks at her. The stone's light flickers across her face. He closes his eyes. Opens them. The stone is a stone.

AUREN
(hoarse)
Thank you.

LYRA
(releasing him)
Don't make me say it again. It's embarrassing.

He lifts the stone from the basin. It lies in his hand, weightless and heavy at once. He wraps it in the cloth and slides it into the satchel with the other two.

INT. THE KINGDOM OF ASH — A BROKEN ARCADE — LATER

They shelter in a broken arcade while the dust-wind passes. Auren sits against a pillar with the satchel at his feet, shivering. Lyra and Tuan are asleep in a corner. Seraphine has gone to check the jeeps.

Torin comes to sit beside Auren. He holds out a flask.

TORIN
Drink.

Auren drinks. It is something stronger than tea.

AUREN
(a cough)
Thanks.

TORIN
(quietly)
What did you see?

AUREN
My mother.

TORIN
(nodding; a long pause)
I saw a boy in a doorway. He was eight. It was raining.

AUREN
(looking at him)
Torin —

TORIN
(speaking to the dark)
My parents died in a flood. I was in an orphanage in a river town. A man came, one winter, a gentle man in a grey coat, and he sat on the end of my bed and said, "You do not have to be anybody's yet." I was fifteen before I understood that he'd been looking for me. Not for a son. For a hand.

AUREN
(very quiet)
Malachar.

TORIN
He gave me a name. A trade. A reason. He never raised his voice.
(his voice cracks)
Everything he said about the Sundering is true. I have heard it a thousand times. I could recite it.

AUREN
(slowly)
Why are you telling me this?

TORIN
(turning)
Because I am going to do something unforgivable, and I would rather you heard why from me.

Auren looks at him for a long, long moment. Something in the quiet between them hardens into knowing.

AUREN
(barely audible)
You told him.

TORIN
Every step.

AUREN
(rising to his feet)
Don't.

TORIN
(rising with him)
I am sorry.

AUREN
(his voice shaking)
You crossed the bridge for me.

TORIN
(a ghost of a smile)
Yes. It was the best thing I have ever done. It is the only reason I am still making this choice slowly.

His hand moves, very fast. A dart hisses from his sleeve. Auren drops the flask and staggers back, hand to his neck, eyes wide. He tries to speak. He tries to reach for the grey stone in his coat. Time does not slow. It does not so much as shiver.

TORIN (CONT'D)
(gently catching him)
You cannot call it while you're sedated. I'm sorry. I am so sorry.

He lowers Auren to the ground. In the corner, Lyra stirs; Torin crosses to her in three strides and presses a second dart to her neck. She wakes just long enough to see his face, and her eyes fill with a fury so total it is almost grief.

LYRA
(slurring)
You...

TORIN
(quietly)
Forgive me.

She goes limp. Tuan, awake now, lunges, and Torin catches him by the shoulder and lays him down like a child.

He takes the satchel. He takes the grey stone from Auren's coat. He stands for a moment over the three sleeping forms in the ash.

In the open archway, a figure appears: Vesper Lund, with Corvin behind her. Vesper looks down at the bodies, at the satchel, at Torin.

VESPER
(a beat)
He said you would.

TORIN
(hollow)
Did he say I'd hate it?

VESPER
(not unkind)
He said that too.

They go out together into the grey dusk. Ash fills their footprints.

EXT. THE KINGDOM OF ASH — THE JEEPS — NIGHT

Seraphine stands by the vehicles, a lantern in her hand, looking at the empty plaza. She has heard nothing. She sees nothing.

Slowly, a figure staggers out of the dark: Auren, one hand clamped to his neck, weaving, half-dragging Lyra, with Tuan stumbling behind.

SERAPHINE
(dropping the lantern)
Auren.

AUREN
(slurring)
He took them. All three.

SERAPHINE
(catching him)
Who?

AUREN
Torin.

SERAPHINE
(very quietly)
Of course.

She lowers him against a jeep. He is shaking now, the shock taking him, and his eyes, which have been dry for two days, finally fill.

AUREN
(broken)
I trusted him.

SERAPHINE
I know.

AUREN
He crossed the bridge.

SERAPHINE
I know, dear.

AUREN
(a hoarse laugh)
I thought we were the good people.

She is very still. The wind lifts her hair. When she speaks, her voice is steady and cold, the voice of someone who has made a decision over four thousand years.

SERAPHINE
We weren't.

He looks up.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
Sit. I owe you something.

INT. A TENT AT THE EDGE OF THE ASH — NIGHT

A lantern. A camp-stove. Two cups. Seraphine sits cross-legged on a blanket across from Auren, who holds a mug in both hands and does not drink. Lyra sleeps in the corner under a coat. Tuan snores.

SERAPHINE
You asked me last night if I'd told you everything. I said no. That was true. I said I'd tell you tomorrow. That was a lie.

AUREN
(quiet)
Say it.

SERAPHINE
I am not called Seraphine.

AUREN
Malachar said.

SERAPHINE
(a small, dry smile)
He always did have the better memory. My name, when I had it, was Eryndor.

A silence so long that the stove ticks.

AUREN
(slowly)
You're one of the seven.

SERAPHINE
I am the last.

AUREN
(his voice rising)
You said you knew her mother. And hers.

SERAPHINE
I knew Ithren herself. I held her hand when she died, in a house by the sea, on the far side of a century you have never heard of. I told her I would find her line. I have been finding it ever since.

AUREN
(getting up; shaking)
You let me think —

SERAPHINE
I know.

AUREN
You let me think we were the good people.

SERAPHINE
(calmly)
Yes.

AUREN
Why?

SERAPHINE
(a long breath)
Because I have been telling myself the same story for four thousand years, and I was afraid that if I said it aloud I would stop being able to believe it.

She lays both hands flat on her knees.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
The Sundering was not Verran's crime. It was ours.

Auren stares.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
The Loom was our design. Seven of us. We built it in secret, under the Conclave's nose, because we were certain we alone could be trusted with it. We told ourselves we were building a guardian for the world. We built a throne.

AUREN
(hoarsely)
A throne.

SERAPHINE
It was never meant to answer to the Conclave. It was meant to answer to us. When Verran learned of it, he seized the dais. We had no plan for that. We had built the only weapon in the world and then left it unguarded, because we had never imagined anyone else would want it.

AUREN
And when it tore the sky —

SERAPHINE
(a barely audible whisper)
We lied. We said we had saved the world. We said the Conclave caused it. We built a Citadel to remember the lie, and for four thousand years the Wardens have stood guard over a fiction.

Auren sinks back onto the blanket. His eyes are very wide.

AUREN
(softly)
Ithren knew.

SERAPHINE
Ithren knew first. She wept for a week. Then she told us we must hide the stones, and never tell anyone what we had really done.

AUREN
(a bitter laugh)
Hold the other end.

SERAPHINE
(startled)
What?

AUREN
She told me. In the vision. Hold the other end.

Seraphine puts a hand to her mouth. For the first time, in all the time he has known her, she cannot speak.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(quiet; piecing it together)
That wasn't an instruction. That was a plea.

SERAPHINE
(whisper)
Yes.

AUREN
She wanted someone to hold the other end of the thing she'd done. Not the stone. The truth.

SERAPHINE
(her voice very small)
Yes.

A silence in which neither moves. Outside, the wind brings the sound of ash falling like soft rain.

AUREN
(at last)
Malachar doesn't know.

SERAPHINE
(startled)
He knows something. He was sleeping when we decided what to say.

AUREN
(slowly)
He thinks the Conclave destroyed his daughter. He thinks we were heroes. He thinks Ithren betrayed him because she was afraid of what he'd do.

SERAPHINE
(a low breath)
Perhaps.

AUREN
If he found out that we...

He stops. The thought arrives in his face, and it is both a weapon and a wound.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(very quietly)
He's repeating it.

SERAPHINE
(beginning to understand)
Auren —

AUREN
(rising; his eyes bright)
He's building the same throne. And he doesn't know.

SERAPHINE
(carefully)
You cannot tell him that. It would break him.

AUREN
(gently)
Then I'd better be very careful how I do it.

INT. THE CHARTERED TOWER, PORT VESSA — THE SAME NIGHT

A rooftop terrace on the city's tallest building, wind-whipped, lit by neon. A TV anchor's voice over a loudspeaker.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
...reports from at least eleven cities of what witnesses describe as "afterimages", buildings that appear and disappear in the sky for several seconds at a time. Authorities say there is no cause for alarm.

Across the harbour, in a sky that is clear and dark and ordinary, a white city flickers into existence: terraces, bridges, canals running uphill. It hangs for three seconds. It is gone.

A crowd on the terrace gasps. A child points. A woman begins to cry without knowing why.

EXT. THE ABBEY SANCTUM — NIGHT

On a hill above a ruined abbey, Malachar stands before five stones on five pedestals. Amber. Brick-red. Grey. Green. Rose-gold. They hum together, a chord of five notes. Torin is beside him, hands clasped, eyes lowered, pale as milk.

MALACHAR
(very gently)
It's begun.

TORIN
(hoarse)
Are you pleased?

MALACHAR
(looking at the sky, where the white city flickers and fades)
I am afraid.

TORIN
(startled)
You?

MALACHAR
Yes. I always am. It is how I know I am doing it properly.

He lays his palm over the grey stone. For an instant, in the stone's cold depth, something flickers: a woman, her hair tied back, a tool-belt at her hip. He draws his hand away as if burned.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
(whisper)
Ithren.

TORIN
(carefully)
What did you see?

MALACHAR
(a long breath)
That she was weeping.

He turns away. On the last pedestal, the sixth, a niche stands empty, and the seventh beyond it.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
Two remain. They are in the Eternal Temple.
(he looks at Torin)
You will take me there.

TORIN
(very quietly)
I will.

MALACHAR
(the paper bird in his hand)
And Torin.

TORIN
Yes?

MALACHAR
Thank you for crossing the bridge.

Torin's face does something terrible and tiny. He bows his head.

EXT. A DESERT ROAD — DAWN

Two jeeps on a long straight road under a bruise-coloured sky. In the first, Seraphine drives, silent, eyes forward. Beside her, Auren holds an empty satchel. In the back seat Lyra and Tuan lean together, asleep.

On the horizon, the thin line of the sky has begun to glow, not with sunrise, but with a seam. A jagged, pale, bright seam, like a lid lifting.

AUREN
(low)
It's the same.

SERAPHINE
(her voice steady)
It is the beginning of the same.

AUREN
How long?

SERAPHINE
Days. If we're lucky.

AUREN
(quietly)
And if we're not?

She does not answer. The seam grows. On the dashboard, in a cup holder, a small thing catches the light: a paper bird, crumpled with age, that nobody remembers putting there.

HOLD on the bird.

END OF ACT TWO
